WEBVTT

1
00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:39.750
Subtitles by explosiveskull

2
00:00:51.986 --> 00:00:56.688
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Here's the dirty
little secret about being rich.</i>

3
00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.761
<i>It's not about the money.</i>

4
00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:02.384
<i>It's about the way people see you.</i>

5
00:01:02.385 --> 00:01:05.585
<i>The way you see yourself.</i>

6
00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:08.209
<i>Fuck money.</i>

7
00:01:08.211 --> 00:01:13.876
<i>For kids like Joe Hunt and me,
being rich is about respect.</i>

8
00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:19.281
<i>(ROCK MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

9
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:20.816
<i>My name is Dean Karney.</i>

10
00:01:20.818 --> 00:01:24.529
<i>I grew up with parents
who were middle class...</i>

11
00:01:24.531 --> 00:01:26.288
<i>the opposite of rich.</i>

12
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.440
<i>But they wanted the best for me...</i>

13
00:01:27.442 --> 00:01:29.328
<i>scraped together tuition
fees to send me...</i>

14
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.784
<i>to the most elite prep
school in Los Angeles.</i>

15
00:01:32.786 --> 00:01:35.153
<i>The Harvard School for Boys...</i>

16
00:01:35.155 --> 00:01:36.592
<i>where I first met Joe...</i>

17
00:01:36.593 --> 00:01:39.184
<i>the scholarship kid with no friends.</i>

18
00:01:39.186 --> 00:01:42.832
<i>The only thing we had in common
is that neither of us belonged.</i>

19
00:01:42.834 --> 00:01:45.328
<i>I learned early on that
the key was to fake it...</i>

20
00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.504
<i>until you make it.</i>

21
00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:50.064
<i>I'd use my charm,
wit, and backhand...</i>

22
00:01:50.066 --> 00:01:52.081
<i>to hang out with the
sons of movie stars...</i>

23
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:55.056
<i>CEO's, actual royalty.</i>

24
00:01:55.058 --> 00:01:57.648
<i>I played the part perfectly.</i>

25
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.344
<i>By the time I was 25...</i>

26
00:01:59.346 --> 00:02:02.993
<i>I signed a deal worth a
quarter of a billion dollars.</i>

27
00:02:02.995 --> 00:02:06.704
<i>I had it all, the house,
the money, the girl.</i>

28
00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:09.553
<i>And I had the best
partner, Joe Hunt.</i>

29
00:02:09.555 --> 00:02:14.257
<i>In Beverly Hills, the
dream isn't a better life.</i>

30
00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:16.912
<i>It's the best life.</i>

31
00:02:16.914 --> 00:02:19.441
<i>So, let me go back.</i>

32
00:02:19.442 --> 00:02:21.808
<i>It all started six years after
high school and let's just...</i>

33
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.561
<i>say that I wasn't exactly
living up to my full potential.</i>

34
00:02:24.563 --> 00:02:26.928
Are you ready to buy a car,
you handsome son of a bitch?

35
00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.400
What do you got?

36
00:02:28.402 --> 00:02:29.616
Well, I got a Bentley.

37
00:02:29.618 --> 00:02:30.832
Boring.

38
00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:32.913
What? You don't like a Bentley?

39
00:02:32.915 --> 00:02:37.396
I got a 1979 IROC driven by
Stallone for three months.

40
00:02:39.954 --> 00:02:41.811
I got a Corvette.

41
00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:44.820
Let me see the 'Vette.

42
00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:52.080
DEAN (V.O.): <i>And there
he was, my old classmate.</i>

43
00:02:52.081 --> 00:02:54.288
<i>He hadn't changed a bit.</i>

44
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.600
If we take the over and the under...

45
00:02:55.602 --> 00:02:59.151
DEAN (V.O.): <i>He was still
the smartest guy in the room.</i>

46
00:02:59.153 --> 00:03:01.458
<i>Still wearing the cheapest suit.</i>

47
00:03:03.953 --> 00:03:05.841
Sorry, ahh...

48
00:03:05.843 --> 00:03:06.927
Corvette.

49
00:03:06.929 --> 00:03:08.658
Mr. Ioku... thank you.

50
00:03:10.098 --> 00:03:12.624
- Dean.
- Joe.

51
00:03:12.626 --> 00:03:14.545
- It's been forever.
- Way too long.

52
00:03:14.547 --> 00:03:16.145
Come here, man.

53
00:03:16.147 --> 00:03:20.240
So, making moves, what have you been up to?

54
00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:22.128
I am working at the Pacific Stock Exchange.

55
00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.400
Wall Street of the West, nice.

56
00:03:24.402 --> 00:03:25.745
And what are you up to?

57
00:03:25.747 --> 00:03:29.584
A bit of this, a bit of that.

58
00:03:29.586 --> 00:03:31.729
Are you living downtown?

59
00:03:31.731 --> 00:03:33.295
I'm still living with my father.

60
00:03:33.297 --> 00:03:34.608
Are you shitting me?

61
00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.113
Are they not paying you?

62
00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.587
Barely.

63
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.729
Is that cash?

64
00:03:43.731 --> 00:03:46.287
Yeah, I just sold the Biltmores a Corvette.

65
00:03:46.289 --> 00:03:48.336
Joe, you shouldn't be
working for these guys.

66
00:03:48.338 --> 00:03:50.705
You should be these guys.
You've gotta get your own thing.

67
00:03:50.707 --> 00:03:53.872
Thanks, I appreciate it.

68
00:03:53.874 --> 00:03:58.737
That would take investors, and
money, and things I don't have.

69
00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:00.595
You know, we would make the perfect team.

70
00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:04.687
Come to Spago tonight. It's
Charlie Bottom's birthday.

71
00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:06.289
We could go talk to him.

72
00:04:06.291 --> 00:04:07.440
Charlie Bottoms from high school?

73
00:04:07.442 --> 00:04:08.624
Yeah.

74
00:04:08.626 --> 00:04:09.680
Yeah, I don't think he's gonna
want to give me his money, Dean.

75
00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.592
Of course, he will. Just do
what you did to those guys.

76
00:04:12.594 --> 00:04:14.353
You know, pitch 'em gold.

77
00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:17.328
I don't think so. These
guys hated me in high school.

78
00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:18.512
I can't.

79
00:04:18.514 --> 00:04:20.177
Yes, Joe, you can.

80
00:04:20.179 --> 00:04:23.184
With your brains and my connections.

81
00:04:23.185 --> 00:04:25.523
Think about it, we could own this town.

82
00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:32.403
That's a yes, right? That
looks like a yes to me.

83
00:04:33.907 --> 00:04:36.241
- Okay.
- Yes, Joe!

84
00:04:36.243 --> 00:04:37.200
Okay.

85
00:04:37.202 --> 00:04:39.281
So it is Spago at 9:00
and it's a costume party.

86
00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:40.976
We'll both go as Reagan's.

87
00:04:40.978 --> 00:04:43.887
You're going come, right? Promise?

88
00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:44.976
Yeah.

89
00:04:44.978 --> 00:04:45.840
Okay, cool.

90
00:04:45.842 --> 00:04:47.763
See you later. It's great to see you, man.

91
00:04:52.818 --> 00:04:55.760
DEAN (V.O.): <i>L.A. was
never a nine-to-five town.</i>

92
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:58.161
<i>Deals were made at night
at places so exclusive...</i>

93
00:04:58.163 --> 00:05:01.168
<i>just getting in meant you
had your checkbook ready.</i>

94
00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.081
<i>Most exclusive of all was
Spago, which is where...</i>

95
00:05:04.083 --> 00:05:08.016
<i>Charlie Bottoms was holding
his 23rd birthday bash.</i>

96
00:05:08.018 --> 00:05:10.097
<i>Halloween in July.</i>

97
00:05:10.099 --> 00:05:12.721
Sorry, kid, it's a private party.

98
00:05:12.723 --> 00:05:15.504
Oh no, I'm invited. I'm
here with Dean Karney.

99
00:05:15.506 --> 00:05:17.839
- Oh yeah, sure you are.
- Yeah.

100
00:05:17.841 --> 00:05:20.304
I pity tha muthafucka'! Back of the line!

101
00:05:20.306 --> 00:05:23.344
No, it is Charlie Bottoms
birthday and I'm joining him.

102
00:05:23.346 --> 00:05:25.522
Make a hole! Make a hole!

103
00:05:34.225 --> 00:05:37.523
Get out of the way.

104
00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:42.771
Ladies.

105
00:05:43.857 --> 00:05:45.231
Dean! Hey!

106
00:05:45.233 --> 00:05:46.384
- DEAN: Joe!
- There he is.

107
00:05:46.386 --> 00:05:50.096
Joe, what are you doing? Come on in.

108
00:05:50.097 --> 00:05:52.017
Charlie will be 24 by
the time we get inside.

109
00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:53.455
Come on, Mr. T, may we?

110
00:05:53.457 --> 00:05:55.313
- You got it.
- Thank you.

111
00:05:55.315 --> 00:05:57.011
- Thank you.
- See you later.

112
00:06:04.594 --> 00:06:06.928
Dude, what happened to
us both being Reagan?

113
00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:12.530
We are both Reagan, I'm Nancy.
It's called edge, come on.

114
00:06:14.418 --> 00:06:15.951
Holy shit.

115
00:06:15.953 --> 00:06:17.267
Right, I told you, dude.

116
00:06:18.962 --> 00:06:21.170
Ladies, it is so good to see you.

117
00:06:23.314 --> 00:06:24.560
You're late.

118
00:06:24.562 --> 00:06:27.089
And you are beautiful.

119
00:06:27.091 --> 00:06:29.968
You are forgiven. Charity case?

120
00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.540
- Old friend.
- I'm Joe.

121
00:06:34.354 --> 00:06:38.161
DEAN (V.O.): <i>The girls at
Spago were out of control.</i>

122
00:06:38.163 --> 00:06:41.455
<i>We ran with a pack of
babes called, The Groovers.</i>

123
00:06:41.457 --> 00:06:43.856
<i>When any new scene hit LA...</i>

124
00:06:43.857 --> 00:06:45.970
<i>they were always on
the crest of the wave.</i>

125
00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.816
<i>♪ They're making
it your intention ♪</i>

126
00:06:52.818 --> 00:06:57.713
<i>♪ Love those dreams
Scheme those schemes ♪</i>

127
00:06:57.715 --> 00:07:02.001
DEAN (V.O.): <i>You don't come to
California for the sunshine.</i>

128
00:07:02.002 --> 00:07:05.011
<i>You come for girls
like Sydney Evans.</i>

129
00:07:16.593 --> 00:07:18.289
Mean Dean.

130
00:07:18.291 --> 00:07:20.944
Hey, Delicious.

131
00:07:20.946 --> 00:07:25.456
- You know her?
- Are you ready?

132
00:07:25.458 --> 00:07:29.297
- What was that for?
- Edge.

133
00:07:29.299 --> 00:07:32.335
There they are! My boys!

134
00:07:32.337 --> 00:07:35.023
Gentlemen, allow me to introduce to you...

135
00:07:35.025 --> 00:07:38.641
our semi-assassinated
President, Joe Hunt...

136
00:07:38.643 --> 00:07:40.369
a very close friend of mine.

137
00:07:40.371 --> 00:07:43.375
Joe, you remember Kyle and Scott Biltmore.

138
00:07:43.377 --> 00:07:45.840
Man, that shit is twisted! I love it!

139
00:07:45.842 --> 00:07:48.432
DEAN (V.O.): <i>This crew was
the epitome of a good time.</i>

140
00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:51.984
<i>Which meant the world
clamored to be around them.</i>

141
00:07:51.986 --> 00:07:55.057
<i>I once watched Tom Cruise's
date excuse herself to go...</i>

142
00:07:55.058 --> 00:07:57.199
<i>to the ladies room, only to
leave out the back door...</i>

143
00:07:57.201 --> 00:07:58.928
<i>with both Biltmores.</i>

144
00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.777
<i>Then there was Charlie Bottoms.</i>

145
00:08:01.778 --> 00:08:03.183
<i>Aside from being blessed
with good looks...</i>

146
00:08:03.185 --> 00:08:06.704
<i>he was a mastermind in business.</i>

147
00:08:06.706 --> 00:08:09.873
<i>And what did all three of these
Harvard alumni have in common?</i>

148
00:08:09.875 --> 00:08:10.961
<i>Money.</i>

149
00:08:10.963 --> 00:08:14.032
<i>These were the richest
kids in Beverly Hills.</i>

150
00:08:14.034 --> 00:08:15.088
Happy birthday, Charlie.

151
00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.328
Thanks for coming.

152
00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.807
<i>(FUNK MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

153
00:08:21.809 --> 00:08:23.634
Got you a gift, Charlie.

154
00:08:31.505 --> 00:08:33.712
What's this, gold?

155
00:08:33.714 --> 00:08:36.431
I've been studying the market
going back forty quarters.

156
00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:39.152
And if we invest in gold right now.

157
00:08:39.154 --> 00:08:40.559
My dad is in gold.

158
00:08:40.561 --> 00:08:42.160
And he says the market's gonna tank.

159
00:08:42.162 --> 00:08:44.177
How is the system going to handle that?

160
00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:45.776
That's why we reverse gold.

161
00:08:45.778 --> 00:08:47.728
We buy and sell a pair of puts and calls...

162
00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.263
and set a spread against volatility.

163
00:08:49.265 --> 00:08:50.641
Hold on, shut up a minute.

164
00:08:50.643 --> 00:08:52.113
Hey, did we go to high school together?

165
00:08:52.115 --> 00:08:53.455
No, no, seriously he looks familiar.

166
00:08:53.457 --> 00:08:55.312
Shit! Yeah! Hey, I fucking know this kid!

167
00:08:55.314 --> 00:08:57.488
He was the one who was thrown
off that debate team, right?

168
00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.280
And then I stuffed the guy in a locker.

169
00:08:59.282 --> 00:09:00.431
We reverse gold.

170
00:09:00.433 --> 00:09:03.087
- Okay, I've got a Macallan...
- We reverse gold.

171
00:09:03.089 --> 00:09:08.850
Macallan 12, a Glenlivet, a Makers.

172
00:09:13.201 --> 00:09:15.120
Happy birthday, Charlie.

173
00:09:15.122 --> 00:09:16.975
Hey!

174
00:09:16.977 --> 00:09:19.663
So, Dean, you got that
ten grand you owe me?

175
00:09:19.665 --> 00:09:20.879
Or my Corvette?

176
00:09:20.881 --> 00:09:23.731
No, it's back at my house. I'll
bring it to you in the morning.

177
00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:35.091
Dude, we gotta get you outta the valley.

178
00:09:46.609 --> 00:09:50.448
Dean, I will never
freeze up like that again.

179
00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:54.706
I know, brother. I believe in you, man.

180
00:09:58.865 --> 00:10:01.042
<i>(TV BLARING)</i>

181
00:10:08.307 --> 00:10:10.771
ED MCMAHON (OVER TV):
<i>Here's... Johnny!</i>

182
00:10:17.202 --> 00:10:21.680
<i>Thank you, the recession
has hit Rodeo Drive.</i>

183
00:10:21.682 --> 00:10:23.600
<i>I saw a guy filling up
his Rolls-Royce today...</i>

184
00:10:23.602 --> 00:10:25.681
<i>at the self-service island.</i>

185
00:10:27.602 --> 00:10:31.023
<i>Yes, and it's depressing
in Beverly Hills...</i>

186
00:10:31.025 --> 00:10:33.873
<i>during a recession.</i>

187
00:10:33.875 --> 00:10:35.280
<i>It's depressing, you
see people lined up...</i>

188
00:10:35.281 --> 00:10:37.999
<i>with food stamps to buy Haagen Daz.</i>

189
00:10:38.001 --> 00:10:40.851
<i>(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

190
00:11:06.289 --> 00:11:07.346
<i>(ALARM BLARING)</i>

191
00:11:13.586 --> 00:11:15.954
Took you long enough.
I called you hours ago.

192
00:11:17.394 --> 00:11:18.768
Have you even slept?

193
00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:24.559
No, Dean, sit down, the
Biltmores, Charlie, all them.

194
00:11:24.561 --> 00:11:27.536
Their names have given them
everything they could ever want.

195
00:11:27.538 --> 00:11:30.064
Except purpose and
responsibility and self-respect.

196
00:11:30.066 --> 00:11:32.752
They've never had to worry about
how to cover a car payment...

197
00:11:32.754 --> 00:11:33.840
or how to make rent.

198
00:11:33.842 --> 00:11:36.529
Things everyone else stays
up nights worrying about.

199
00:11:36.531 --> 00:11:38.193
So, of course, when I showed
up with a flake of gold...

200
00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:40.848
and asked them to invest, it
didn't mean anything to them.

201
00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:43.024
These guys, they don't care
about market analysis...

202
00:11:43.026 --> 00:11:44.367
or projections or any of that shit.

203
00:11:44.369 --> 00:11:45.455
They want to be part of something?

204
00:11:45.457 --> 00:11:46.511
Exactly.

205
00:11:46.513 --> 00:11:49.104
And every business needs
its founding priniciples.

206
00:11:49.105 --> 00:11:51.536
You've heard of Steve Jobs?

207
00:11:51.538 --> 00:11:54.095
Yeah, of course.

208
00:11:54.097 --> 00:11:55.312
Remind me?

209
00:11:55.314 --> 00:11:58.191
Steve Jobs, he is 26 years old.
He's living the American dream.

210
00:11:58.193 --> 00:12:02.224
He dropped out of college and
now he's worth $149 million.

211
00:12:02.226 --> 00:12:03.344
How?

212
00:12:03.346 --> 00:12:06.863
Because he took computer
technology and made it simple.

213
00:12:06.865 --> 00:12:09.840
He's selling the future.

214
00:12:09.842 --> 00:12:11.091
Read this.

215
00:12:12.434 --> 00:12:15.056
Paradox Philosophy.

216
00:12:15.058 --> 00:12:18.352
Turn bad into good,
even wrong becomes right.

217
00:12:18.354 --> 00:12:22.767
In the right context, you could
take yesterday's failures...

218
00:12:22.769 --> 00:12:24.881
and turn them into tomorrow's successes.

219
00:12:24.882 --> 00:12:26.928
This is rad.

220
00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.328
What's the BBC?

221
00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:31.664
It's the name of our company.

222
00:12:31.666 --> 00:12:33.808
What does it stand for?

223
00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.215
Remember that poker game...

224
00:12:35.217 --> 00:12:37.680
we could never get into in high school?

225
00:12:37.682 --> 00:12:38.736
No one will know.

226
00:12:38.738 --> 00:12:42.995
But, to us, it'll mean
attaining the unattainable.

227
00:12:50.994 --> 00:12:52.431
What's that?

228
00:12:52.433 --> 00:12:56.274
Kyle's apology. Ten grand.

229
00:12:57.618 --> 00:13:00.624
Holy shit.

230
00:13:00.626 --> 00:13:04.047
Turn it into 20 and everyone
of Kyle's friends and family...

231
00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:06.161
will be lining up to give us their money.

232
00:13:06.162 --> 00:13:08.208
Between word of our success, and this...

233
00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:12.912
it'll spread across Beverly
Hills like wild fire.

234
00:13:12.914 --> 00:13:14.448
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Before you
turn your nose up...</i>

235
00:13:14.449 --> 00:13:15.951
<i>at a measly ten grand...</i>

236
00:13:15.953 --> 00:13:18.864
<i>remember, these were
the Reagan Years.</i>

237
00:13:18.866 --> 00:13:21.807
<i>Gas was 91 cents a gallon.
Cigarettes were a buck.</i>

238
00:13:21.809 --> 00:13:24.431
<i>And $10,000 was pretty close
to the yearly income...</i>

239
00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:25.712
<i>of an average family.</i>

240
00:13:25.713 --> 00:13:28.431
<i>Joe took Kyle Biltmore's
money and invested in gold.</i>

241
00:13:28.433 --> 00:13:30.959
<i>In three weeks it was
up thirty five percent.</i>

242
00:13:30.961 --> 00:13:32.848
<i>We couldn't lose.</i>

243
00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:33.968
These look great.

244
00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:37.040
And you wanted to use staples.

245
00:13:37.042 --> 00:13:39.023
Okay, so we gotta be at the
Biltmores by 6:00, right?

246
00:13:39.025 --> 00:13:40.527
Let's hit the racks down at Nate and Al's.

247
00:13:40.529 --> 00:13:41.647
I gotta check the closing price on gold.

248
00:13:41.649 --> 00:13:42.735
Well, aren't we up?

249
00:13:42.737 --> 00:13:44.335
Yeah, we were up this morning.

250
00:13:44.337 --> 00:13:45.616
But I gotta check the closing price.

251
00:13:45.618 --> 00:13:48.145
Okay, I just gotta make one stop first.

252
00:13:55.089 --> 00:13:58.193
I just sold this guy a car last weekend.

253
00:13:59.698 --> 00:14:01.519
Nice, huh?

254
00:14:01.521 --> 00:14:02.800
Yeah.

255
00:14:02.802 --> 00:14:04.691
<i>(DOOR BELL RINGS)</i>

256
00:14:06.066 --> 00:14:08.463
- Mr. Karney, he's expecting you.
- Hi.

257
00:14:08.465 --> 00:14:10.736
RON: I want you in the front
row at that court house...

258
00:14:10.737 --> 00:14:13.231
all right, on his face, with your camera...

259
00:14:13.233 --> 00:14:15.248
the moment the judge reads
those charges, all right?

260
00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:18.095
Every other journalist is gonna
want the same fucking shot!

261
00:14:18.097 --> 00:14:21.008
All right? Come on, get up,
now! I want you to play rough.

262
00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:22.736
I want you to claw their fucking eyes out.

263
00:14:22.738 --> 00:14:24.943
I want you to tell them you
work for Barbara Walters.

264
00:14:24.945 --> 00:14:26.864
Tell 'em you are Barbara
Walters if you have to.

265
00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:29.104
This press badge will get you
access everywhere, all right?

266
00:14:29.106 --> 00:14:31.951
Listen to me, front row. That
close up of John DeLorean?

267
00:14:31.953 --> 00:14:33.904
I don't want you to miss
it! Do you understand?

268
00:14:33.906 --> 00:14:36.048
Yes, sir.

269
00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:37.520
Ah yes.

270
00:14:37.522 --> 00:14:40.912
Bon jour, combien allez-vous
payer des photo de Jon Delorean?

271
00:14:40.914 --> 00:14:42.384
(PHONE BEEPING)

272
00:14:42.386 --> 00:14:45.295
Ah oui, merci.

273
00:14:45.296 --> 00:14:47.087
Nigel, we're a go.

274
00:14:47.089 --> 00:14:50.671
Yes. Buy GM the moment that
those charges are announced.

275
00:14:50.673 --> 00:14:55.248
Yes, at 52 and a half.
That's right, thank you.

276
00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.263
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Ron Levin
was a force of nature.</i>

277
00:14:57.265 --> 00:14:58.831
Oh, hi, Giselle. Hi, it's Ronnie.

278
00:14:58.833 --> 00:15:00.688
Yeah, I'd like three chopped salads.

279
00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:01.873
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Made a
fortune on Wall Street...</i>

280
00:15:01.874 --> 00:15:03.984
<i>with the Rothschild Group
before going west...</i>

281
00:15:03.986 --> 00:15:06.927
<i>to dabble in any deal
he found interesting...</i>

282
00:15:06.929 --> 00:15:08.847
<i>lucrative, or both.</i>

283
00:15:08.849 --> 00:15:09.998
No, you're staying for lunch.

284
00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.775
Yes, and some Tiramisu with
three spoons. Thank you so much.

285
00:15:13.777 --> 00:15:17.744
AUTOMATED VOICE: <i>Warning!
Warning! Bullshit alert!</i>

286
00:15:17.746 --> 00:15:19.731
Dean, show me.

287
00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.464
All right.

288
00:15:28.466 --> 00:15:33.231
1962 original patina.

289
00:15:33.233 --> 00:15:34.226
Solid chrome.

290
00:15:36.273 --> 00:15:38.895
Yeah, but Dean, these
are not in mint condition.

291
00:15:38.897 --> 00:15:40.687
These have not been stored properly.

292
00:15:40.689 --> 00:15:43.440
I am not paying $2000! No.

293
00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:46.803
I will pay you at most
$600 for the whole set.

294
00:15:49.233 --> 00:15:51.919
And who is your friend, Dean?

295
00:15:51.921 --> 00:15:53.808
This is my partner, Ron.

296
00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:55.727
Do you remember Joe Hunt,
I was telling you about.

297
00:15:55.729 --> 00:15:59.887
Oh, right, yes, Dean told
me you're a trader of sorts?

298
00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:01.935
Yeah, that's right, Mr. Levin.
It's a pleasure to meet you.

299
00:16:01.937 --> 00:16:03.727
I notice you have the afternoon edition.

300
00:16:03.729 --> 00:16:05.392
- You mind if I take a look?
- No, go right ahead.

301
00:16:05.394 --> 00:16:08.559
- Thank you.
- Joe is a genius.

302
00:16:08.561 --> 00:16:11.058
This guy had like a six...

303
00:16:17.361 --> 00:16:19.055
What?

304
00:16:19.057 --> 00:16:23.759
Rough day in the market?

305
00:16:23.761 --> 00:16:26.479
No, not at all.

306
00:16:26.481 --> 00:16:28.623
You know, Mr. Levin, it's
really an honor meeting you.

307
00:16:28.625 --> 00:16:29.904
I'd love for you and I to sit down...

308
00:16:29.906 --> 00:16:31.502
and speak through my investment strategy.

309
00:16:31.504 --> 00:16:32.943
I think you might find it very interesting.

310
00:16:32.945 --> 00:16:36.592
Are you familiar with the
commodities market, Mr. Levin?

311
00:16:36.594 --> 00:16:39.599
Am I familiar with the
commodities market, Dean?

312
00:16:39.601 --> 00:16:42.835
Hm... why don't you tell me.

313
00:16:49.073 --> 00:16:51.024
Holy shit.

314
00:16:51.026 --> 00:16:53.456
That can't be real.

315
00:16:53.458 --> 00:16:54.927
What is it?

316
00:16:54.929 --> 00:16:57.616
It's a Bulla... Sumerian's used it.

317
00:16:57.618 --> 00:17:00.144
They put those tokens
inside their clay pot...

318
00:17:00.146 --> 00:17:01.583
and marked the day a
commodity was delivered.

319
00:17:01.585 --> 00:17:03.472
It's... it's 5,000 years old.

320
00:17:03.474 --> 00:17:05.551
No, it's actually 6000 years old.

321
00:17:05.553 --> 00:17:07.985
You're looking at the world's
first futures contract.

322
00:17:09.105 --> 00:17:10.703
Must be worth a fortune.

323
00:17:10.705 --> 00:17:12.559
Do you want to hold it?

324
00:17:12.561 --> 00:17:14.512
Are you kidding me?

325
00:17:14.514 --> 00:17:16.752
No.

326
00:17:16.754 --> 00:17:19.728
Just be careful. 'Cause
it's worth a lot of money.

327
00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:25.712
Shit! Oh my God.

328
00:17:25.714 --> 00:17:28.494
Oh shit!

329
00:17:28.496 --> 00:17:31.184
- Dean, go find a broom.
- Okay.

330
00:17:31.185 --> 00:17:34.065
Please tell me that was
insured. I'm so sorry.

331
00:17:39.793 --> 00:17:43.344
Insured? That was priceless.

332
00:17:43.346 --> 00:17:46.191
There are only 17 Bullas
on the entire planet.

333
00:17:46.193 --> 00:17:50.384
And every single one of them...

334
00:17:50.385 --> 00:17:53.906
is locked in a museum.

335
00:17:56.849 --> 00:17:58.895
It's a fake.

336
00:17:58.897 --> 00:18:00.143
But you thought it was an original.

337
00:18:00.145 --> 00:18:01.071
Yeah.

338
00:18:01.073 --> 00:18:05.391
And why? Because the
perception of reality...

339
00:18:05.392 --> 00:18:08.239
is more real than reality itself.

340
00:18:08.241 --> 00:18:10.639
<i>(DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

341
00:18:10.641 --> 00:18:12.496
DEAN (V.O.): <i>The Biltmore's
parents had taken off...</i>

342
00:18:12.498 --> 00:18:13.456
<i>to Europe for the summer.</i>

343
00:18:13.458 --> 00:18:15.983
<i>So the boys had turned their home...</i>

344
00:18:15.985 --> 00:18:18.480
<i>into their own private clubhouse.</i>

345
00:18:18.481 --> 00:18:23.023
<i>The place was always crawling
with Beverly Hills royalty.</i>

346
00:18:23.024 --> 00:18:24.975
Mean Dean gonna get this
show on the road, or what?

347
00:18:24.977 --> 00:18:27.856
Probably boning in the bathroom.

348
00:18:27.858 --> 00:18:29.459
Go get him and hurry it up!

349
00:18:30.896 --> 00:18:33.327
Dean, we took a hit in the market today.

350
00:18:33.329 --> 00:18:34.735
What? How bad?

351
00:18:34.737 --> 00:18:37.136
We wiped out.

352
00:18:37.138 --> 00:18:39.215
- All of it?
- Half.

353
00:18:39.217 --> 00:18:40.336
The rest is still in the account.

354
00:18:40.338 --> 00:18:44.016
Yo, Dean, your guests are waiting!

355
00:18:44.018 --> 00:18:48.079
Uh, just give us a second, Kyle.

356
00:18:48.081 --> 00:18:50.863
Wait, what are you doing?

357
00:18:50.865 --> 00:18:53.007
I'm giving Kyle his money back.

358
00:18:53.009 --> 00:18:54.095
What, you're gonna tell him now?

359
00:18:54.097 --> 00:18:56.336
After the presentation,
in private, yes, I have to.

360
00:18:56.338 --> 00:19:00.272
What presentation, Joe? There
is no way these guys will buy...

361
00:19:00.274 --> 00:19:02.320
our pitch if Kyle's
bitching about a wipe out.

362
00:19:02.322 --> 00:19:03.983
I know, but I don't have a choice.

363
00:19:03.985 --> 00:19:05.807
All you have is your rep.

364
00:19:05.809 --> 00:19:07.503
Dean, I'm just gonna
be honest with the guy.

365
00:19:07.505 --> 00:19:09.040
He knew there were risks
when he got involved.

366
00:19:09.042 --> 00:19:11.983
He doesn't know.

367
00:19:11.985 --> 00:19:14.095
What do you mean, he doesn't know?

368
00:19:14.097 --> 00:19:16.782
There was no apologies?

369
00:19:16.784 --> 00:19:18.512
You gave me his money without him knowing?

370
00:19:18.514 --> 00:19:19.952
Sorry, I wanted to put wind in your sails.

371
00:19:19.954 --> 00:19:21.041
I should have told you the truth.

372
00:19:21.043 --> 00:19:27.119
But think how many legends
started exactly like this.

373
00:19:27.121 --> 00:19:29.966
The Kennedys were rum runners
before they ran the world.

374
00:19:29.968 --> 00:19:33.105
This is our chance, come on.

375
00:19:39.089 --> 00:19:40.912
Gentlemen, if I can have
your attention, please?

376
00:19:40.914 --> 00:19:43.440
Thank you for coming by tonight.

377
00:19:43.442 --> 00:19:45.679
Some of you may remember my friend...

378
00:19:45.681 --> 00:19:48.079
Joe Hunt from High School.

379
00:19:48.081 --> 00:19:51.599
We spoke to some of you guys
at Spago around three weeks ago.

380
00:19:51.601 --> 00:19:52.751
If you can't remember the night...

381
00:19:52.753 --> 00:19:56.080
it's the night Charlie has been
trying to forget ever since.

382
00:19:56.082 --> 00:19:57.648
No way.

383
00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.143
But I'm not the guy to talk
to you about the minutia...

384
00:20:00.145 --> 00:20:01.839
of why we are here.

385
00:20:01.841 --> 00:20:06.642
So, Joe? You want to...

386
00:20:08.273 --> 00:20:11.824
Want to tell them about our proposal?

387
00:20:11.826 --> 00:20:14.383
DEAN (V.O.): <i>You want
to talk about a paradox?</i>

388
00:20:14.385 --> 00:20:17.263
<i>Joe's bad break was our lucky day.</i>

389
00:20:17.265 --> 00:20:19.471
<i>When he got caught in my lie...</i>

390
00:20:19.473 --> 00:20:22.960
<i>we broke through to
a whole, new level.</i>

391
00:20:22.962 --> 00:20:27.699
<i>All he had to do was
cross one tiny line.</i>

392
00:20:30.481 --> 00:20:31.824
Gentlemen!

393
00:20:31.825 --> 00:20:34.480
You are the young Turks
of L.A. heirs to empires...

394
00:20:34.482 --> 00:20:36.079
sons of scions.

395
00:20:36.081 --> 00:20:39.664
But, what would you do
to earn it on your own?

396
00:20:39.666 --> 00:20:43.312
To look your fathers in
the eye, not for a handout.

397
00:20:43.314 --> 00:20:47.151
But with a handful of cash,
that you pulled down, with this!

398
00:20:47.153 --> 00:20:50.991
Dump the opportunity at your
fingertips? Fuck no! Seize it.

399
00:20:50.993 --> 00:20:55.376
Now is the time to own your own destiny...

400
00:20:55.378 --> 00:21:00.463
and become part of something great!

401
00:21:00.465 --> 00:21:01.518
The BBC.

402
00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:04.976
What does BBC stand for?

403
00:21:04.978 --> 00:21:06.063
Exactly.

404
00:21:06.065 --> 00:21:07.982
Within that question lies our hook?

405
00:21:07.984 --> 00:21:12.879
Each investor will ask, "What is
BBC?" Are we a holding company?

406
00:21:12.881 --> 00:21:16.974
Are we an investment
pool? Are we a social club?

407
00:21:16.976 --> 00:21:18.576
All the above.

408
00:21:18.578 --> 00:21:21.775
Buy on mystery. Sell on history.

409
00:21:21.777 --> 00:21:24.047
I like it.

410
00:21:24.049 --> 00:21:25.744
Sounds like old money.

411
00:21:25.746 --> 00:21:28.206
Fuck old money!

412
00:21:28.208 --> 00:21:32.367
How about we make some new money?

413
00:21:32.369 --> 00:21:35.791
We all stand on the verge of
wildly profitable ventures...

414
00:21:35.793 --> 00:21:37.968
in this room alone.

415
00:21:37.969 --> 00:21:41.808
Scott, you bought a Benz
in Munich, drove it...

416
00:21:41.809 --> 00:21:46.382
across Europe and sold it right
here for twice what you paid.

417
00:21:46.384 --> 00:21:49.679
That is a hundred percent profit.

418
00:21:49.681 --> 00:21:52.591
Charlie, how many times
have you advised your dad...

419
00:21:52.593 --> 00:21:56.911
on energy stocks, only for
him to make the big move.

420
00:21:56.913 --> 00:22:00.879
And Kyle, you believed
in my trading system...

421
00:22:00.881 --> 00:22:02.735
to the tune of $10,000.

422
00:22:02.737 --> 00:22:04.591
- You what?
- I did?

423
00:22:04.593 --> 00:22:07.470
Yes! You did!

424
00:22:07.472 --> 00:22:09.807
And here's your first disbursement check!

425
00:22:09.809 --> 00:22:13.326
Wait, five thousand
dollars? Is this what's left?

426
00:22:13.328 --> 00:22:17.006
No, that is profit.

427
00:22:17.008 --> 00:22:19.087
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Instead of
admitting we lost five grand...</i>

428
00:22:19.089 --> 00:22:20.464
The rest is still in gold.

429
00:22:20.465 --> 00:22:23.151
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Joe told
them we made five grand.</i>

430
00:22:23.153 --> 00:22:26.992
Fifty percent, in three weeks.

431
00:22:26.994 --> 00:22:28.560
Yeah.

432
00:22:28.562 --> 00:22:30.001
God damn.

433
00:22:35.312 --> 00:22:36.558
This is just a loan.

434
00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.342
Okay.

435
00:22:39.344 --> 00:22:41.262
What do we do if he
asks for his money back?

436
00:22:41.264 --> 00:22:43.791
Look, Dean, this weekend?

437
00:22:43.793 --> 00:22:45.103
You're gonna scrounge up every dime...

438
00:22:45.105 --> 00:22:47.982
so we can reinvest and make his money back.

439
00:22:47.984 --> 00:22:50.544
You got it, Joe.

440
00:22:50.546 --> 00:22:53.520
So guys, think it over, let us know.

441
00:22:53.522 --> 00:22:54.798
We'll see you later.

442
00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:56.242
Bullshit.

443
00:22:58.641 --> 00:23:01.518
You guys are coming out with us tonight.

444
00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:05.199
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

445
00:23:05.201 --> 00:23:07.087
Yo, Tim!

446
00:23:07.089 --> 00:23:10.608
This cash machine, right here,
is my new best friend, Joe Hunt.

447
00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:13.231
If he ever waits in line again,
and I'll sic the wolf on you.

448
00:23:13.233 --> 00:23:15.440
He doesn't mean that,
Tim. Pleasure to meet you.

449
00:23:15.442 --> 00:23:18.287
Welcome to Spago, Mr. Hunt.

450
00:23:18.289 --> 00:23:19.536
Thanks, Tim.

451
00:23:19.538 --> 00:23:20.498
Hello, Tim.

452
00:23:21.649 --> 00:23:23.375
Ladies.

453
00:23:23.377 --> 00:23:25.007
Gentlemen, if I may.

454
00:23:25.009 --> 00:23:27.311
To Paradox Philosophy.

455
00:23:27.312 --> 00:23:29.935
Wait, wait, wait, Para-what?

456
00:23:29.937 --> 00:23:31.024
Paradox Philosophy.

457
00:23:31.026 --> 00:23:33.903
It's our theory that is
outlined in each prospectus.

458
00:23:33.905 --> 00:23:37.006
Three weeks ago, Joe blew
his pitch to you guys.

459
00:23:37.008 --> 00:23:40.878
And tonight, he made you five grand, Kyle.

460
00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:41.999
That is a paradox.

461
00:23:42.001 --> 00:23:43.696
Winning by losing.

462
00:23:43.698 --> 00:23:46.351
Seeing the paradox
allows you to do things...

463
00:23:46.353 --> 00:23:48.495
that you thought you couldn't.

464
00:23:48.497 --> 00:23:51.631
Cross lines you said you wouldn't.

465
00:23:51.633 --> 00:23:53.199
For example, Charlie.

466
00:23:53.201 --> 00:23:56.432
What is the worst thing
you can imagine doing?

467
00:23:56.434 --> 00:24:00.047
The worst? Murder?

468
00:24:00.049 --> 00:24:01.839
Okay, perfect.

469
00:24:01.841 --> 00:24:05.390
So could you kill someone
for a million dollars?

470
00:24:05.392 --> 00:24:06.671
No.

471
00:24:06.672 --> 00:24:07.919
See?

472
00:24:07.921 --> 00:24:09.646
That's a line you can't cross.

473
00:24:09.648 --> 00:24:11.087
Yeah, but who would?

474
00:24:11.089 --> 00:24:12.174
Well, no one here for sure, but...

475
00:24:12.176 --> 00:24:13.456
Kyle, what if you walked into a room...

476
00:24:13.458 --> 00:24:15.023
and your mother was being raped?

477
00:24:15.025 --> 00:24:16.911
Would you kill then?

478
00:24:16.913 --> 00:24:20.111
If my mother was being
raped? Yes, well, fuck yeah.

479
00:24:20.113 --> 00:24:22.000
Yeah, but under those
circumstances, anybody would.

480
00:24:22.002 --> 00:24:23.214
Okay, so then change the circumstances...

481
00:24:23.216 --> 00:24:24.559
and you'll cross that line...

482
00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:27.471
the line you said you wouldn't cross.

483
00:24:27.473 --> 00:24:30.736
So by shifting your perspective
you can justify anything.

484
00:24:30.738 --> 00:24:33.199
Justify, that implies right and wrong.

485
00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:35.310
But if you reconcile your perspective...

486
00:24:35.312 --> 00:24:38.321
then right and wrong become relative.

487
00:24:40.049 --> 00:24:44.272
Chateau la fete Rothschild,
1959 finest Bordeaux.

488
00:24:44.274 --> 00:24:45.423
Hey, yo, we didn't order that.

489
00:24:45.425 --> 00:24:49.519
This is complements of Chef
Puck's closest friend, sir.

490
00:24:49.521 --> 00:24:52.142
Dean. Excuse me, boys.

491
00:24:52.144 --> 00:24:54.639
He asks that you go alone.

492
00:24:54.641 --> 00:24:56.689
That's okay, come on, Dean.

493
00:24:59.377 --> 00:25:01.487
I think he's awful.
You think he's talented?

494
00:25:01.489 --> 00:25:02.575
Yes, I do.

495
00:25:02.577 --> 00:25:03.919
Oh my God, his art is...

496
00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:05.167
Graffiti, first of all, looks bourgeois.

497
00:25:05.169 --> 00:25:06.222
No, it doesn't.

498
00:25:06.224 --> 00:25:07.055
And all of his art looks
like it's finger painting.

499
00:25:07.056 --> 00:25:08.942
I love finger painting, it's so mature.

500
00:25:08.944 --> 00:25:10.639
Oh, that's why everyone
thinks he's a genius.

501
00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:11.856
Because you love him,
and you say it publicly.

502
00:25:11.857 --> 00:25:12.879
WARHOL: So?

503
00:25:12.881 --> 00:25:14.606
But I'm telling you this,
no one is gonna remember...

504
00:25:14.608 --> 00:25:16.494
Jean-Michel Basket...

505
00:25:16.496 --> 00:25:17.711
Basquiat.

506
00:25:17.713 --> 00:25:19.566
Basquiat... in like, 15
minutes. Forget about it.

507
00:25:19.568 --> 00:25:21.520
- Oh, Joe, hello.
- Mr. Levin.

508
00:25:21.522 --> 00:25:24.655
You came to say hi, and
you brought Dean anyway.

509
00:25:24.657 --> 00:25:27.054
Mr. Levin, I want to say
thank you for the wine.

510
00:25:27.056 --> 00:25:28.622
And but of course I had to
bring my partner with me.

511
00:25:28.624 --> 00:25:30.287
Well, I guess I'll have to respect that.

512
00:25:30.289 --> 00:25:32.399
But if you don't call me Ron,
I might have to murder you.

513
00:25:32.401 --> 00:25:36.111
And this is my friend, Andy. Andy, Joe.

514
00:25:36.113 --> 00:25:37.647
It is a pleasure to meet you.

515
00:25:37.649 --> 00:25:39.182
WARHOL: Nice to meet you.

516
00:25:39.184 --> 00:25:42.287
Andy is in town doing, are you ready?

517
00:25:42.289 --> 00:25:45.616
An episode of The Love
Boat, with Milton Berle.

518
00:25:45.618 --> 00:25:48.143
Oh, and apparently Milton
Berle has a Love Boat...

519
00:25:48.145 --> 00:25:50.478
between his legs.

520
00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:52.143
WARHOL: It's true.

521
00:25:52.145 --> 00:25:54.351
Any dick contest, he takes
out just enough to win.

522
00:25:54.353 --> 00:25:56.688
- It's as big as a salami.
- It is?

523
00:25:56.690 --> 00:25:57.935
- It's big, it's like huge.
- Wow.

524
00:25:57.937 --> 00:26:00.562
Do you like salami, Joe?

525
00:26:02.225 --> 00:26:07.406
Who doesn't, dude?

526
00:26:07.408 --> 00:26:08.559
So you know, Ron.

527
00:26:08.561 --> 00:26:11.310
We just cleared fifty
percent in three weeks.

528
00:26:11.312 --> 00:26:13.199
Really? And I thought for sure
you took a horrible beating...

529
00:26:13.201 --> 00:26:15.023
this afternoon.

530
00:26:15.025 --> 00:26:16.462
No.

531
00:26:16.464 --> 00:26:19.406
I shorted gold hard and
made out like a bandit.

532
00:26:19.408 --> 00:26:21.327
Well, that is quite a return.

533
00:26:21.329 --> 00:26:22.543
I'm serious.

534
00:26:22.544 --> 00:26:24.302
We really should get together
and talk about you investing.

535
00:26:24.304 --> 00:26:26.992
You see I knew it the
moment I saw you, Joe.

536
00:26:26.993 --> 00:26:31.730
You're a hustler, just like me.

537
00:26:33.296 --> 00:26:35.215
Thank you so much for coming to say hi.

538
00:26:35.217 --> 00:26:37.646
You will excuse us, won't you, Joe?

539
00:26:37.648 --> 00:26:38.703
Of course, of course.

540
00:26:38.705 --> 00:26:45.198
Joe, this is for you. Sugar,
because you are so sweet.

541
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:46.545
Thank you so much.

542
00:26:52.977 --> 00:26:55.278
I say we write two checks right now, bro.

543
00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:56.622
Think so?

544
00:26:56.624 --> 00:26:58.798
I fucking know so, bro!

545
00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:03.954
Hey this goose shits golden eggs.

546
00:27:10.002 --> 00:27:11.983
Hey Joe! Joe!

547
00:27:11.985 --> 00:27:14.095
Hey, can we have a word real quick?

548
00:27:14.097 --> 00:27:17.582
Our dad is always riding our ass
saying "Think about the future."

549
00:27:17.584 --> 00:27:21.106
Well here is our thoughts.

550
00:27:23.504 --> 00:27:25.552
Kill it.

551
00:27:28.465 --> 00:27:29.935
- Yes! Yes!
- Go make us some money.

552
00:27:29.937 --> 00:27:31.728
Have a good night.

553
00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:35.183
Hey, your name is Sydney,
right? And you are an artist.

554
00:27:35.185 --> 00:27:37.198
I work for one.

555
00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:39.118
No, I hear you are a
really talented artist.

556
00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:41.647
Dean told me. Right?

557
00:27:41.649 --> 00:27:43.759
We have an investment company.

558
00:27:43.761 --> 00:27:46.575
And I was just talking to Andy Warhol.

559
00:27:46.576 --> 00:27:49.582
Do you know Andy Warhol,
have you heard of him?

560
00:27:49.584 --> 00:27:50.447
Come see me!

561
00:27:50.449 --> 00:27:53.007
Obviously yeah, you have heard of him.

562
00:27:53.009 --> 00:27:54.095
Yeah.

563
00:27:54.097 --> 00:27:57.040
But, I'm you know, trying
to land people like Andy.

564
00:27:57.042 --> 00:27:58.959
But I don't know anything about the scene.

565
00:27:58.961 --> 00:28:00.879
It looks like you are getting
to know the scene pretty well.

566
00:28:00.881 --> 00:28:02.030
No, the art scene.

567
00:28:02.032 --> 00:28:05.038
I don't know the first thing about it.

568
00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:10.319
So, I was thinking,
maybe you could tutor me?

569
00:28:10.321 --> 00:28:11.439
Tutor you?

570
00:28:11.441 --> 00:28:13.360
You know, give me a crash course in art.

571
00:28:13.362 --> 00:28:14.479
I don't know anything about it.

572
00:28:14.481 --> 00:28:18.479
I got this for you.

573
00:28:18.481 --> 00:28:23.698
It could be down payment on a crash course.

574
00:28:24.817 --> 00:28:26.162
I'll think about it.

575
00:28:29.969 --> 00:28:32.334
<i>(ENGINE STARTING)</i>

576
00:28:32.336 --> 00:28:35.695
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Joe sold
the world that night.</i>

577
00:28:35.697 --> 00:28:39.439
<i>And the Biltmores'
money put us to work.</i>

578
00:28:39.441 --> 00:28:40.879
<i>Before the Euro, there
were these things...</i>

579
00:28:40.881 --> 00:28:42.512
<i>called Deutsche Marks.</i>

580
00:28:42.513 --> 00:28:44.718
<i>And dollars bought German
shit for dirt cheap...</i>

581
00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:47.311
<i>that we could sell here
for a hefty markup.</i>

582
00:28:47.312 --> 00:28:49.232
<i>We made a deal with
Scott's guy in Munich...</i>

583
00:28:49.233 --> 00:28:51.570
<i>and the BBC entered the gray market.</i>

584
00:28:53.393 --> 00:28:55.311
<i>(ENGINE REVVING)</i>

585
00:28:55.313 --> 00:29:01.614
<i>The M635-CSi was the nastiest
thing on the Autobahn.</i>

586
00:29:01.616 --> 00:29:05.103
<i>You have heard of an M5, or an M3?</i>

587
00:29:05.105 --> 00:29:08.303
<i>This was the first
street-legal M class ever.</i>

588
00:29:08.305 --> 00:29:11.568
<i>You couldn't get them in America.</i>

589
00:29:11.569 --> 00:29:13.618
<i>Which meant everyone
in L.A. wanted one.</i>

590
00:29:15.505 --> 00:29:17.423
Give me the keys.

591
00:29:17.425 --> 00:29:18.450
They're in the car.

592
00:29:26.384 --> 00:29:28.175
(CLEARS THROAT)

593
00:29:28.177 --> 00:29:31.150
<i>(DEVICE WHIRRING)</i>

594
00:29:31.152 --> 00:29:32.974
Have you driven a new car before?

595
00:29:32.976 --> 00:29:34.894
This car is worth more than my apartment.

596
00:29:34.896 --> 00:29:37.550
Are you sure you want to
put miles in this puppy...

597
00:29:37.552 --> 00:29:38.863
before it passes smog?

598
00:29:38.865 --> 00:29:40.878
<i>(ENGINE STARTS)</i>

599
00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:43.600
Oh yeah.

600
00:29:47.408 --> 00:29:49.681
<i>(DOOR BELL RINGS)</i>

601
00:29:56.913 --> 00:30:00.142
So, the BBC has a motor pool now.

602
00:30:00.144 --> 00:30:03.023
Come on, I gotta get dressed.
So, let me get this straight.

603
00:30:03.025 --> 00:30:06.288
Germany, pass smog for five...

604
00:30:06.289 --> 00:30:08.782
and sell them in Beverly Hills for $70,000?

605
00:30:08.784 --> 00:30:10.703
$80,000. It's a five-fold profit.

606
00:30:10.705 --> 00:30:13.583
Ron, you can share if you go in on BBC.

607
00:30:13.585 --> 00:30:15.310
I'm thinking twenty grand.

608
00:30:15.312 --> 00:30:18.063
Fifteen grand.

609
00:30:18.065 --> 00:30:19.214
Ten, we can start small.

610
00:30:19.216 --> 00:30:22.223
I spent ten thousand dollars
on dinner the other night, Joe.

611
00:30:22.225 --> 00:30:24.143
You could buy a Chrysler for that.

612
00:30:24.145 --> 00:30:26.542
Which is exactly why I
called it the Chrysler Dinner.

613
00:30:26.544 --> 00:30:28.143
Which is a steal compared
to the Lincoln dinner...

614
00:30:28.145 --> 00:30:30.543
I'm throwing tonight at Mr. Chows.

615
00:30:30.545 --> 00:30:34.446
Joe, you think too small.

616
00:30:34.448 --> 00:30:35.631
Maybe if you are gonna pitch me...

617
00:30:35.633 --> 00:30:38.703
you ought to think about
adding a digit or two.

618
00:30:38.705 --> 00:30:43.278
Because when I go in, I go all in.

619
00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.198
Okay, get your checkbook out.

620
00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:47.246
No, no, no, it's gonna
take more than a 635-CSi...

621
00:30:47.248 --> 00:30:48.624
to get my check book out.

622
00:30:51.184 --> 00:30:53.646
I invest in relationships, Joe.

623
00:30:53.648 --> 00:30:54.734
Not deals.

624
00:30:54.736 --> 00:30:56.686
People, not stock.

625
00:30:56.688 --> 00:30:58.703
- You read our prospectus?
- Yes.

626
00:30:58.705 --> 00:31:00.623
And I made some notes in the margins.

627
00:31:00.625 --> 00:31:03.630
You're kind of all over the place, Joe.

628
00:31:03.632 --> 00:31:06.767
Low risk spreads on high commodities.

629
00:31:06.768 --> 00:31:09.679
Performance BMW's in the gray market.

630
00:31:09.681 --> 00:31:11.151
And a gold mill?

631
00:31:11.153 --> 00:31:13.010
Ron, that gold mill is
the deal of the century.

632
00:31:15.185 --> 00:31:17.839
I'll tell you what I did
like. Go to the first page.

633
00:31:17.841 --> 00:31:23.537
That is interesting. That
is the essence of success.

634
00:31:25.615 --> 00:31:29.358
Reminded me a lot of when I
got in hot water with the SEC.

635
00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:30.223
Oh, don't worry about it.

636
00:31:30.225 --> 00:31:31.727
Anybody who knows what they're doing...

637
00:31:31.729 --> 00:31:34.223
eventually gets flagged by the SEC.

638
00:31:34.225 --> 00:31:36.143
You should wear it as a badge of honor.

639
00:31:36.145 --> 00:31:38.063
I had been working at Rothchild for a year.

640
00:31:38.065 --> 00:31:40.559
When suddenly I found
myself thrown in front...

641
00:31:40.561 --> 00:31:43.439
of a discipline committee and
was forced to defend myself.

642
00:31:43.441 --> 00:31:46.607
And I had nothing.

643
00:31:46.609 --> 00:31:50.670
21 stories and none of
them good, so I came clean.

644
00:31:50.672 --> 00:31:53.007
They take your license?

645
00:31:53.009 --> 00:31:54.830
No, I mean they could have,
they should have, but no.

646
00:31:54.832 --> 00:31:57.711
Because what I was doing
wasn't illegal... yet.

647
00:31:57.713 --> 00:32:02.798
I was ahead of the law. I mean,
it was unethical and corrupt.

648
00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:04.719
Immoral if you believe
in that sort of thing.

649
00:32:04.721 --> 00:32:08.335
But it wasn't illegal, so they had nothing.

650
00:32:08.337 --> 00:32:11.119
So guess what they did.

651
00:32:11.121 --> 00:32:15.151
They asked me to write the code...

652
00:32:15.153 --> 00:32:19.022
to prohibit the kind of
trading that I had been doing.

653
00:32:19.024 --> 00:32:21.646
How brilliant is that?

654
00:32:21.648 --> 00:32:25.679
That hearing made me with the SEC.

655
00:32:25.681 --> 00:32:28.590
And I realized that day that...

656
00:32:28.592 --> 00:32:33.809
sometimes the truth is the best lie.

657
00:32:35.664 --> 00:32:36.686
Why come to me?

658
00:32:36.688 --> 00:32:39.086
Your father certainly
has his wealthier friends.

659
00:32:39.088 --> 00:32:41.038
But none he respects more.

660
00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:42.190
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Our first
investors became...</i>

661
00:32:42.192 --> 00:32:43.759
<i>our number one sales team.</i>

662
00:32:43.761 --> 00:32:44.814
Just the kind of investor...

663
00:32:44.816 --> 00:32:47.150
we wanna do business with at the BBC.

664
00:32:47.152 --> 00:32:49.583
It's not at all about money, Mr. Baxter.

665
00:32:49.585 --> 00:32:52.142
DEAN (V.O.): <i>And Joe's
strategy put us on the map.</i>

666
00:32:52.144 --> 00:32:54.542
It's about integrity.

667
00:32:54.543 --> 00:32:56.623
DEAN (V.O.): <i>The only
difference between a nobody...</i>

668
00:32:56.625 --> 00:33:01.583
<i>and a player, is an address.</i>

669
00:33:01.585 --> 00:33:03.982
286 horsepower... Uncle Hank,

670
00:33:03.984 --> 00:33:07.728
and you are the only one
at the LACC driving one.

671
00:33:09.744 --> 00:33:11.982
Dad, you know I don't
take investing lightly.

672
00:33:11.984 --> 00:33:13.903
And I've never come to you before.

673
00:33:13.905 --> 00:33:15.823
I mean, I really think this is a winner.

674
00:33:15.825 --> 00:33:17.103
BBC is ground breaking.

675
00:33:17.105 --> 00:33:19.023
Doing things that is revolutionary.

676
00:33:19.025 --> 00:33:21.262
And the principles which Granddad had

677
00:33:21.264 --> 00:33:22.863
when he started the family business.

678
00:33:22.865 --> 00:33:25.199
I like the sign. The
bold makes a statement.

679
00:33:25.201 --> 00:33:27.119
It is bold and beautiful, right?

680
00:33:27.121 --> 00:33:28.911
If you'd like to come through...

681
00:33:28.913 --> 00:33:30.766
this will be our conference room.

682
00:33:30.768 --> 00:33:32.719
I'm not just a member
of the BBC, Mr. Baxter.

683
00:33:32.721 --> 00:33:34.318
Line two.

684
00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:34.926
Yeah.

685
00:33:34.928 --> 00:33:35.855
Yeah, yeah, ask my father.

686
00:33:35.857 --> 00:33:39.599
I believe in Joe Hunt.
I believe in his vision.

687
00:33:39.601 --> 00:33:41.359
This is the easiest money
you are ever going to make.

688
00:33:41.361 --> 00:33:46.510
I mean, Joe got me fifty
percent in three weeks.

689
00:33:46.512 --> 00:33:47.758
Fifty percent in three weeks.

690
00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:49.358
Total Mad Maxing the market.

691
00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:51.502
I'm talking... Blows the doors off

692
00:33:51.504 --> 00:33:52.911
anything you'd make at Shearson Lehman!

693
00:33:52.913 --> 00:33:54.542
Kind of sounds too good to be true.

694
00:33:54.544 --> 00:33:56.719
It looks true to me.

695
00:33:56.721 --> 00:33:58.031
DEAN (V.O.): <i>We pulled in everyone.</i>

696
00:33:58.033 --> 00:34:01.167
<i>BBC was no longer our game.
It belonged to all of us.</i>

697
00:34:01.169 --> 00:34:04.046
<i>It was a family, which
was the secret to the BBC.</i>

698
00:34:04.048 --> 00:34:05.230
- I'm in.
- All right.

699
00:34:05.232 --> 00:34:06.414
DEAN (V.O.): <i>We wanted
members with friends...</i>

700
00:34:06.416 --> 00:34:08.270
<i>and families who were
titans of the west coast.</i>

701
00:34:08.272 --> 00:34:09.393
I'm in.

702
00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.138
I'm in.

703
00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:18.766
- I'm in.
- How much?

704
00:34:18.768 --> 00:34:21.966
Call.

705
00:34:21.968 --> 00:34:23.246
Boys!

706
00:34:23.248 --> 00:34:25.169
Come on, guys. Come on.

707
00:34:27.056 --> 00:34:28.270
Gentlemen...

708
00:34:28.272 --> 00:34:30.287
DEAN (V.O.): <i>But Ron didn't
trust outside brokers.</i>

709
00:34:30.289 --> 00:34:32.365
<i>Insisted on using his own.</i>

710
00:34:32.367 --> 00:34:33.359
RECEPTIONIST (OVER SPEAKER): <i>Good
morning, Morgan Brokerage.</i>

711
00:34:33.361 --> 00:34:34.414
<i>How may I help you?</i>

712
00:34:34.416 --> 00:34:35.759
Hello, Frank Piedmont please.

713
00:34:35.761 --> 00:34:36.657
RECEPTIONIST (OVER
SPEAKER): <i>One moment.</i>

714
00:34:38.193 --> 00:34:39.246
FRANK (OVER SPEAKER):
<i>Frank Piedmont.</i>

715
00:34:39.248 --> 00:34:41.327
Hello, Frank, I'm here with Joe Hunt.

716
00:34:41.329 --> 00:34:43.406
FRANK (OVER SPEAKER):
<i>Hey, Ron, hey, Joe.</i>

717
00:34:43.408 --> 00:34:45.007
Good afternoon, Mr. Piedmont.

718
00:34:45.009 --> 00:34:47.022
Ron has told me about your
unique trading system...

719
00:34:47.023 --> 00:34:49.006
in the gold commodities market.

720
00:34:49.008 --> 00:34:50.799
I'm impressed. That's not easy to do.

721
00:34:50.801 --> 00:34:53.486
Most people lose their shirts
if they stay in gold too long.

722
00:34:53.488 --> 00:34:54.606
I'm sorry to be so direct...

723
00:34:54.608 --> 00:34:57.138
but I would like to check
the balance in the account.

724
00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:10.702
Frank, do you mind
saying that one more time?

725
00:35:10.704 --> 00:35:12.303
FRANK (OVER SPEAKER): <i>At closing
market today, the account...</i>

726
00:35:12.305 --> 00:35:14.510
<i>balance was four million,
one hundred thousand...</i>

727
00:35:14.511 --> 00:35:16.337
<i>two dollars and five cents.</i>

728
00:35:21.713 --> 00:35:22.703
Thank you, Frank.

729
00:35:22.705 --> 00:35:25.968
Joe will be back in
touch very soon, I'm sure.

730
00:35:27.889 --> 00:35:30.094
Remember the butterflies
in your gut right now.

731
00:35:30.096 --> 00:35:34.095
And know that is the show.

732
00:35:34.097 --> 00:35:36.015
You sell a BMW, you pay your rent.

733
00:35:36.017 --> 00:35:37.870
You refine a tanker full of crude...

734
00:35:37.872 --> 00:35:39.726
you cover your overhead for a year.

735
00:35:39.728 --> 00:35:43.343
But if you perform on the
account I just handed you...

736
00:35:43.345 --> 00:35:49.422
then your lives will never be the same.

737
00:35:49.424 --> 00:35:53.519
Think big and you will be big.

738
00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:56.078
Unless, of course, I got you boys wrong.

739
00:35:56.080 --> 00:36:00.655
I mean, after all, what
does the BBC stand for again?

740
00:36:00.657 --> 00:36:01.646
Oh right.

741
00:36:01.648 --> 00:36:04.590
Bitching Boring Crew?

742
00:36:04.592 --> 00:36:09.457
The Brainless Boneheaded Cartel?

743
00:36:12.177 --> 00:36:21.966
Or are you, the Billionaire Boys Club?

744
00:36:21.967 --> 00:36:23.725
DEAN (V.O.): <i>One Ron said
think big, we took it to heart.</i>

745
00:36:23.727 --> 00:36:26.958
<i>From now on, every
check that came in...</i>

746
00:36:26.960 --> 00:36:28.559
<i>went towards more luxury.</i>

747
00:36:28.561 --> 00:36:31.473
<i>Which meant more loans.</i>

748
00:36:32.624 --> 00:36:33.838
Billionaire...

749
00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:35.345
Or bust.

750
00:36:36.752 --> 00:36:39.886
As one of our first
investors, Mr. Baxter...

751
00:36:39.888 --> 00:36:41.872
I wanted to come by personally
and deliver this check.

752
00:36:43.921 --> 00:36:47.886
50 percent return, just like we promised.

753
00:36:47.887 --> 00:36:51.758
I don't normally invest, but
I have to say that the BBC...

754
00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:55.247
has really struck a cord with me.

755
00:36:55.249 --> 00:36:57.519
Especially watching
young men like yourselves.

756
00:36:57.521 --> 00:37:01.295
I can only hope my sons
follow in your footsteps.

757
00:37:01.297 --> 00:37:06.829
Which is why I've decided.

758
00:37:06.831 --> 00:37:09.104
And invest it for them, in the BBC.

759
00:37:11.983 --> 00:37:13.902
Hell, I even took out a second mortgage.

760
00:37:13.904 --> 00:37:17.359
I'm hooked. I'm hooked.

761
00:37:17.361 --> 00:37:18.766
<i>You're probably
wondering why we take</i>

762
00:37:18.768 --> 00:37:22.414
<i>this money when we just got
four mil from Ron Levin.</i>

763
00:37:22.416 --> 00:37:25.199
<i>We weren't actually holding
Ron's money in our hands.</i>

764
00:37:25.201 --> 00:37:28.045
<i>We were just authorized to
trade that money on his behalf.</i>

765
00:37:28.047 --> 00:37:30.128
<i>We were paper rich, but cash poor.</i>

766
00:37:37.521 --> 00:37:39.565
Dean, look at this.

767
00:37:39.567 --> 00:37:42.063
That's his kid's college fund.

768
00:37:42.065 --> 00:37:44.878
That is the house that they live in.

769
00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:47.567
We can't keep promising
fifty percent returns.

770
00:37:47.569 --> 00:37:49.710
We are committing fraud.

771
00:37:49.712 --> 00:37:51.570
Should we give it back?

772
00:37:55.408 --> 00:38:00.845
Honestly, Joe, the only
difference between high risk...

773
00:38:00.847 --> 00:38:05.266
investing and a Ponzi
scheme, is how it all ends.

774
00:38:07.503 --> 00:38:11.313
That check is just one more
reason, to pull it all off.

775
00:38:15.248 --> 00:38:17.870
You know what beats a kids college fund?

776
00:38:17.872 --> 00:38:20.045
Four kids college funds.

777
00:38:20.047 --> 00:38:24.078
We are gonna make Baxter so much money...

778
00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:27.118
he pays off both his mortgages.

779
00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:28.977
And sends every kid to grad school.

780
00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:39.150
Hi, I'm meeting Burt Langdon.

781
00:38:39.151 --> 00:38:41.487
Oh, Mr. Langdon just called.

782
00:38:41.489 --> 00:38:43.341
He wanted me to tell you that
he had a family emergency...

783
00:38:43.343 --> 00:38:45.934
and that he will call you
tomorrow and he was very sorry.

784
00:38:45.936 --> 00:38:47.569
Oh, thanks.

785
00:38:55.919 --> 00:39:01.454
Okay, so who has got the big
schlong, is it Scott or Kyle?

786
00:39:01.456 --> 00:39:04.335
What? You are sleeping with both of them!

787
00:39:04.337 --> 00:39:06.317
No, but it is kind of a disaster.

788
00:39:06.319 --> 00:39:08.400
Something is very wrong with you guys.

789
00:39:11.727 --> 00:39:12.975
Are you following me?

790
00:39:12.977 --> 00:39:16.078
What? No, I had a meeting here.

791
00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:17.902
I think he is stalking you.

792
00:39:17.904 --> 00:39:20.656
No, I swear. You can ask the hostess.

793
00:39:23.151 --> 00:39:24.687
We're kidding.

794
00:39:24.689 --> 00:39:26.063
I have to go meet Dean.

795
00:39:26.065 --> 00:39:27.566
No, you don't.

796
00:39:27.568 --> 00:39:29.199
Such a nightmare when your
girlfriends hate your boyfriend.

797
00:39:29.201 --> 00:39:30.351
Oh...

798
00:39:30.353 --> 00:39:33.999
Come on, I'll take you to
see Scotty boy or is it Kyle?

799
00:39:34.001 --> 00:39:35.886
Whoever is home.

800
00:39:35.888 --> 00:39:37.167
- Love you, babes.
- Bye.

801
00:39:37.169 --> 00:39:39.090
- See you tonight at the gallery.
- Bye.

802
00:39:43.023 --> 00:39:46.381
So, do you not like Dean for Q?

803
00:39:46.383 --> 00:39:48.301
Or do you just not like Dean at all?

804
00:39:48.303 --> 00:39:49.966
I am not a fan of Mean Dean.

805
00:39:49.968 --> 00:39:51.022
Mean Dean?

806
00:39:51.024 --> 00:39:52.942
Why does everyone call him that?

807
00:39:52.944 --> 00:39:54.926
You don't know?

808
00:39:54.928 --> 00:39:57.230
In third grade he burned down
the game room at Camp Tawonga...

809
00:39:57.232 --> 00:39:58.958
and blamed my cousin Jake for it.

810
00:39:58.960 --> 00:40:00.845
Jake got sent home for the entire summer...

811
00:40:00.847 --> 00:40:03.566
and Dean won the camp tennis championship.

812
00:40:03.568 --> 00:40:04.942
That's not a coincidence.

813
00:40:04.944 --> 00:40:07.501
You are talking about third grade.

814
00:40:07.503 --> 00:40:09.421
A leopard doesn't change its spots.

815
00:40:09.423 --> 00:40:11.087
Acutally a leopard does change its spots...

816
00:40:11.089 --> 00:40:14.255
according to Scientific American.

817
00:40:14.257 --> 00:40:16.175
Are you always this ahh, literal?

818
00:40:16.177 --> 00:40:17.870
Oh yeah, always.

819
00:40:17.872 --> 00:40:22.253
Do you want some?

820
00:40:22.255 --> 00:40:23.214
No thanks.

821
00:40:23.216 --> 00:40:24.686
I'm good.

822
00:40:24.688 --> 00:40:27.118
Do you not drink?

823
00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:28.977
I've actually never had a sip.

824
00:40:30.736 --> 00:40:31.695
Tell me more.

825
00:40:31.697 --> 00:40:34.285
What do you want to know?

826
00:40:34.287 --> 00:40:35.374
Everything.

827
00:40:35.376 --> 00:40:38.158
Wow, okay.

828
00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:39.662
Yeah, I'm from the Valley.

829
00:40:39.664 --> 00:40:43.215
I went to Harvard School for
Boys on a full scholarship.

830
00:40:43.217 --> 00:40:45.197
My mom left when I was 15.

831
00:40:45.199 --> 00:40:48.719
So I choose to stay with
my dad. Okay, your turn.

832
00:40:48.721 --> 00:40:51.503
I grew up in Beverly Hills.

833
00:40:51.505 --> 00:40:54.509
I went to an all-girls high school.

834
00:40:54.511 --> 00:40:58.127
My parents are still together,
happily if there's such a thing.

835
00:40:58.129 --> 00:41:00.143
Have you always wanted to be an artist?

836
00:41:00.145 --> 00:41:02.062
Always is a strong word, but yeah.

837
00:41:02.064 --> 00:41:04.270
Yeah, I guess so.

838
00:41:04.272 --> 00:41:07.150
Cool. And you said you had a show tonight?

839
00:41:07.151 --> 00:41:08.398
Not me, my boss.

840
00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:11.981
JOE: Oh, is his stuff any good?

841
00:41:11.983 --> 00:41:14.702
I'll let you be the judge.

842
00:41:14.704 --> 00:41:16.496
Was that an invitation?

843
00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:28.142
Hey, Dad.

844
00:41:28.144 --> 00:41:30.190
Where did you get all the fancy clothes?

845
00:41:30.192 --> 00:41:33.391
At Armani, they're nice, right?

846
00:41:33.393 --> 00:41:35.566
With the way things are going...

847
00:41:35.568 --> 00:41:37.679
we're not gonna have to worry
about anything ever again.

848
00:41:37.681 --> 00:41:39.885
- I'm gonna set you up.
- You're gonna set me up?

849
00:41:39.887 --> 00:41:40.846
Yeah.

850
00:41:40.848 --> 00:41:41.774
BBC is really taking off.

851
00:41:41.776 --> 00:41:45.518
I'm going to get you out of this shit hole.

852
00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.566
I thought that the one good
thing I did in my life was to

853
00:41:47.568 --> 00:41:49.133
get you an quality education.

854
00:41:49.135 --> 00:41:51.053
So you can have a better life than me.

855
00:41:51.055 --> 00:41:52.974
But had I known that you were
going to emulate these people

856
00:41:52.976 --> 00:41:56.461
that think they are better than us.

857
00:41:56.463 --> 00:41:58.925
Joe, it's my experience
that if you are honest

858
00:41:58.927 --> 00:42:00.527
in business, things don't happen this fast.

859
00:42:00.529 --> 00:42:02.129
You worry too much.

860
00:42:04.817 --> 00:42:07.921
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

861
00:42:11.824 --> 00:42:15.888
Dude, this suit is lethal.

862
00:42:19.121 --> 00:42:23.342
Joe, gold was down 15 percent at the bell.

863
00:42:23.344 --> 00:42:25.040
It'll correct.

864
00:42:26.767 --> 00:42:28.814
What do you mean the installers broke it!

865
00:42:28.816 --> 00:42:31.406
- I'm sorry...
- Just get out of my sight!

866
00:42:31.408 --> 00:42:32.752
Now!

867
00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:06.285
You look beautiful.

868
00:43:06.287 --> 00:43:07.824
Hardly.

869
00:43:10.735 --> 00:43:12.593
Nice suit.

870
00:43:14.768 --> 00:43:16.430
Look, if you ever want
to be taken seriously...

871
00:43:16.432 --> 00:43:19.022
in the art world, you'll run.

872
00:43:19.024 --> 00:43:20.718
Whoa, whoa, whoa, Sydney.

873
00:43:20.720 --> 00:43:23.470
Seriously, you are going to
let this guy bring you down?

874
00:43:23.472 --> 00:43:24.462
This guy?

875
00:43:24.464 --> 00:43:25.678
Look at his glasses.

876
00:43:25.680 --> 00:43:27.598
He looks like Clark Kent on Quaaludes.

877
00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:31.245
They are very fashionable glasses.

878
00:43:31.247 --> 00:43:33.934
Seriously, Sydney.

879
00:43:33.936 --> 00:43:36.846
How many people would be here
tonight, if it wasn't for you?

880
00:43:36.848 --> 00:43:41.678
How many of these ideas, did he run by you?

881
00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:43.600
He's lucky to have you.

882
00:43:45.359 --> 00:43:46.640
So lucky...

883
00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:50.320
Come on, let's go fix this thing.

884
00:43:57.231 --> 00:43:59.184
I think I got it.

885
00:44:02.063 --> 00:44:05.616
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

886
00:44:13.009 --> 00:44:15.085
You didn't have to stay.

887
00:44:15.087 --> 00:44:17.134
You kidding me, this is rad.

888
00:44:17.136 --> 00:44:19.470
<i>♪ Looking from
the window above ♪</i>

889
00:44:19.472 --> 00:44:25.166
<i>♪ It's like a story of
love Can you hear me ♪</i>

890
00:44:25.168 --> 00:44:31.406
<i>♪ Came back only yesterday
Moving farther away ♪</i>

891
00:44:31.408 --> 00:44:34.797
<i>♪ Want you near me ♪</i>

892
00:44:34.799 --> 00:44:38.701
<i>♪ All I needed was
the love you gave ♪</i>

893
00:44:38.703 --> 00:44:42.853
<i>♪ All I needed for another day ♪</i>

894
00:44:42.855 --> 00:44:46.703
<i>♪ And all I ever knew ♪</i>

895
00:44:46.704 --> 00:44:48.622
<i>♪ Only you ♪</i>

896
00:45:11.664 --> 00:45:14.670
This is our ticket to
the deal of the century.

897
00:45:14.672 --> 00:45:16.781
It was originally built
for gold, but Joe thinks...

898
00:45:16.783 --> 00:45:19.247
we make a fortune using
it in the energy sector.

899
00:45:19.249 --> 00:45:22.509
So there's a company in
Chicago, it's called Cogenco.

900
00:45:22.511 --> 00:45:24.462
They turn oil-burning furnaces into coal.

901
00:45:24.464 --> 00:45:26.029
But they are in Chapter 11...

902
00:45:26.031 --> 00:45:28.366
because they still haven't
figured out how to grind it.

903
00:45:28.368 --> 00:45:31.886
So you want to use a gold mill for coal?

904
00:45:31.888 --> 00:45:35.919
This deal, is worth a quarter
of a billion dollars...

905
00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:37.965
in the first three years.

906
00:45:37.967 --> 00:45:40.686
And we are using your money to buy it.

907
00:45:40.688 --> 00:45:42.893
Told ya, Ron, Joe is a genius.

908
00:45:42.895 --> 00:45:44.045
Yeah, I know you told me, Dean.

909
00:45:44.046 --> 00:45:46.254
But it is a forgone conclusion
that everything you say...

910
00:45:46.256 --> 00:45:47.697
is completely full of shit.

911
00:45:53.456 --> 00:45:55.149
Is that the bouncer?

912
00:45:55.151 --> 00:45:56.910
That is my new bodyguard.

913
00:45:56.912 --> 00:45:58.157
Why do you need a bodyguard?

914
00:45:58.159 --> 00:46:00.237
Exactly what we want each share-holder

915
00:46:00.239 --> 00:46:01.839
to wonder when we walk in.

916
00:46:01.840 --> 00:46:03.437
Tim, it's good to see you!

917
00:46:03.439 --> 00:46:04.688
Good to be seen.

918
00:46:06.575 --> 00:46:09.646
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Cogenco had
big debts, $3 million worth.</i>

919
00:46:09.648 --> 00:46:12.718
<i>We agreed to pay them off using
Ron's money and in exchange</i>

920
00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:15.566
<i>the board agreed to sell us
the company and merge it.</i>

921
00:46:15.568 --> 00:46:17.870
<i>All we had to do now...</i>

922
00:46:17.872 --> 00:46:18.957
Ladies and gentlemen.

923
00:46:18.959 --> 00:46:20.430
<i>...convince the shareholders.</i>

924
00:46:20.432 --> 00:46:26.221
I stand before you today with a proposal.

925
00:46:26.223 --> 00:46:27.950
So what's the question?

926
00:46:27.952 --> 00:46:32.558
The 1,000-pound elephant in this room...

927
00:46:32.559 --> 00:46:35.533
is 'how old are these
boys?' Well let me tell ya.

928
00:46:35.535 --> 00:46:37.966
I'm 24.

929
00:46:37.967 --> 00:46:40.397
My Chief Operating Officer, Dean Karny.

930
00:46:40.399 --> 00:46:42.061
He's 25.

931
00:46:42.063 --> 00:46:45.838
In fact, there isn't a single
member of BBC Consolidated...

932
00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:48.941
that's over the age of 30.

933
00:46:48.943 --> 00:46:50.477
And that should scare you.

934
00:46:50.479 --> 00:46:52.974
You're damn right it does.

935
00:46:52.976 --> 00:46:58.733
Because you have no clue how
we solved in a matter of months

936
00:46:58.735 --> 00:47:04.334
what 10 years of R&D has
landed you $3 million in debt

937
00:47:04.336 --> 00:47:07.149
and one vote away from bankruptcy.

938
00:47:07.151 --> 00:47:09.710
Inaction right now will render you extinct!

939
00:47:09.712 --> 00:47:15.408
But guts right now will make you rich!

940
00:47:17.487 --> 00:47:18.829
The choice is yours.

941
00:47:18.831 --> 00:47:24.110
DEAN (V.O.): <i>Three hours after
walking into that room...</i>

942
00:47:24.112 --> 00:47:26.349
<i>we left with a deal
for two corporations...</i>

943
00:47:26.351 --> 00:47:28.302
<i>twelve million in stock options...</i>

944
00:47:28.304 --> 00:47:31.470
<i>and patents worth a
quarter billion dollars.</i>

945
00:47:31.472 --> 00:47:34.669
<i>Fuck Google. Fuck
Facebook. Fuck them all!</i>

946
00:47:34.671 --> 00:47:36.846
<i>We were the first.</i>

947
00:47:36.848 --> 00:47:39.758
This house was inspired by Richard Myren.

948
00:47:39.760 --> 00:47:41.454
Three bedrooms, two baths.

949
00:47:41.456 --> 00:47:44.942
It can be rented unfurnished
or fully furnished...

950
00:47:44.944 --> 00:47:46.545
that's up to you.

951
00:47:51.407 --> 00:47:54.062
What are we doing here?

952
00:47:54.063 --> 00:47:55.249
What do you think, Dean?

953
00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:02.221
We owe disbursements to everyone...

954
00:48:02.223 --> 00:48:04.045
and no one is reinvesting.

955
00:48:04.047 --> 00:48:05.135
How can we afford this place?

956
00:48:05.137 --> 00:48:08.526
Ron is wiring us our half
of the profits next week.

957
00:48:08.528 --> 00:48:13.328
Then we own Congenco and
a deal worth $250 million.

958
00:48:16.592 --> 00:48:18.097
Wow.

959
00:48:21.008 --> 00:48:22.925
Remember when you said
you wanted to get me...

960
00:48:22.927 --> 00:48:25.613
out of the valley, Dean.

961
00:48:25.615 --> 00:48:27.473
Yeah.

962
00:48:30.800 --> 00:48:33.840
Don't tell me you don't want to
wake up to this every morning.

963
00:48:38.511 --> 00:48:41.392
<i>(MUSIC PLAYING)</i>

964
00:48:45.232 --> 00:48:47.085
Do you realize the kind of whales...

965
00:48:47.087 --> 00:48:49.902
we can reel in with this place?

966
00:48:49.904 --> 00:48:53.517
This place legitimizes
us on the ultimate level.

967
00:48:53.519 --> 00:48:56.206
A couple of turntables by the jacuzzi.

968
00:48:56.208 --> 00:48:59.661
Paint that pool purple and this
place goes off like an ICBM.

969
00:48:59.663 --> 00:49:03.886
And you, my friend, can say
goodbye to Mean Dean forever.

970
00:49:03.888 --> 00:49:06.381
I hate that fucking name.

971
00:49:06.383 --> 00:49:10.861
<i>♪ Home it's where I want to be ♪</i>

972
00:49:10.863 --> 00:49:14.766
<i>♪ Pick me up and
turn me around ♪</i>

973
00:49:14.768 --> 00:49:18.861
<i>♪ I feel numb born
with a weak heart ♪</i>

974
00:49:18.863 --> 00:49:22.766
<i>♪ I guess I must be having fun ♪</i>

975
00:49:22.768 --> 00:49:24.205
But, Ron, the check still hasn't landed.

976
00:49:24.207 --> 00:49:25.581
Oh, that's strange, they were wiring it...

977
00:49:25.583 --> 00:49:26.701
from New York this week.

978
00:49:26.703 --> 00:49:28.974
I'll have to call Frank on Monday.

979
00:49:28.976 --> 00:49:32.142
Listen, it's been a month
and I need the money.

980
00:49:32.144 --> 00:49:34.189
I'm in hot water, okay.

981
00:49:34.191 --> 00:49:35.597
I spread myself thin with this place...

982
00:49:35.599 --> 00:49:37.485
and the office and Congenco.

983
00:49:37.487 --> 00:49:38.766
And I've sort of been...

984
00:49:38.768 --> 00:49:42.349
Diverting investments to pay expenses.

985
00:49:42.351 --> 00:49:47.086
Yes. Wait, you knew?

986
00:49:47.088 --> 00:49:48.238
Joe, I'm from Wall Street.

987
